    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d5">

                <pb facs="0018" n="6" sameAs="frus:frus1942v05/pg6"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.Consultation (3)/105: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Chile</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Bowers</hi>)<anchor n="2" xml:id="d5-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-25T15:00:00-05:00">December 25, 1941—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>633. The Department is advised that the Minister of Foreign Affairs of
                        Argentina<anchor n="3" xml:id="d5-f2"/> expects to have conferences with the
                    Ministers of Foreign Affairs of Chile,<anchor n="4" xml:id="d5-f3"/>
                        Paraguay,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d5-f4"/> Bolivia,<anchor n="6" xml:id="d5-f5"
                    /> and possibly Peru<anchor n="7" xml:id="d5-f6"/> on their way through Buenos
                    Aires to the meeting of Foreign Ministers in Rio<anchor n="8" xml:id="d5-f7"/>
                    with a view to securing their support of the “Argentine position”.</p>
                <p>Although the Department does not yet have any precise information as to the
                    “Argentine position” recent indications of Argentine policy would appear to
                    leave considerable room for its modification until it comes up to the level
                    toward which the policy of the countries which have not yet declared war or
                    broken off relations seems to be evolving.</p>
                <p>Accordingly you are instructed to find some appropriate opening for suggesting to
                    the Foreign Minister that since there are a number of projects, resolutions, and
                    policies now under consideration by the Governments of the American Republics he
                    may wish to defer taking any position with respect to them, at least in a
                    definitive way, prior to the convening of the conference and until an
                    opportunity has been afforded of learning fully there the views of all the other
                    Governments.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d5-f1" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d5-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated on the same date as telegram No. 553 to the Ambassador in Peru, No. 155
                    to the Minister in Paraguay, and No. 278 to the Chargé in Bolivia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f2" n="3" xml:id="d5-f2-content">
                <p>Enrique Ruiz Guiñazú.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f3" n="4" xml:id="d5-f3-content">
                <p>Juan Rossetti.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f4" n="5" xml:id="d5-f4-content">
                <p>Luis A. Argaña.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f5" n="6" xml:id="d5-f5-content">
                <p>Eduardo Anze Matienzo.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f6" n="7" xml:id="d5-f6-content">
                <p>Alfredo Solf y Muro.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d5-f7" n="8" xml:id="d5-f7-content">
                <p>For the membership of the American delegation to the Conference, see Department
                    of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, January 3, 1942, p. 12.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
